Conformance
Id	Clause
conformance.1	This specification defines one class of product, a converter, which reads an OTLP JSON Lines file and writes an MTSV file. A converter is a generator of [MTSV-DRAFT], and MUST meet that document's requirements for generators.
conformance.2	A converter conforms if it writes, for every conformance input it must convert, the sheets of the expected file: the same sheets, in the same order, with the same sheet names, headers and rows; and if it rejects every conformance input it must reject.
conformance.3	The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "NOT RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this specification are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 [BCP14] when, and only when, they appear in all capitals, as shown here.
conformance.4	The Rules and the Sheets are normative. The first part of a rule's Id names the level of the input it governs. A rule names each source it follows in brackets, as [OTLP]; the References list them, each marked normative or informative. This specification has no optional features: where a source leaves a choice to the implementation, a rule closes it.
conformance.5	Sheet, sheet name, header, field and line are as defined in [MTSV-DRAFT]; row, column and cell as in [CSVW], a row being written as one line of fields. Member, name and value are as in [RFC8259]; property, schema and type as in [JSON-SCHEMA-07]; attribute as in [OTEL-COMMON]. An address is a JSON Pointer [RFC6901] from the file: its first reference token is the zero-based index of a line, and its other tokens point within that line's value. The eight are the attributes of set.1.
Rules
Id	Rule
file.1	The input is an OTLP JSON Lines file [OTEL-FILE-EXPORTER]: UTF-8 with no byte order mark, one JSON value per line, each line ended by LF, or CRLF, the terminator after the last line optional [JSONL], each line a TracesData, MetricsData or LogsData in the OTLP JSON encoding [OTLP], one kind of data per file [OTEL-FILE-EXPORTER]. A converter MUST convert a TracesData or LogsData line whole, an envelope with no contents included [OTLP]. The eight are recorded on spans and events [OTEL-GENAI], so a MetricsData line holds none of them, and a converter MUST write no rows for it. A converter MUST read an invalid UTF-8 sequence in a string as U+FFFD [OTLP].
file.2	The lines of a file are in no guaranteed order [OTEL-FILE-EXPORTER]. A converter MUST read them in the file's order, and MUST NOT sort them.
file.3	A converter MUST ignore members with unknown names [OTLP], and MUST read a member of an OTLP message whose value is null as absent [PROTOJSON]. Of the members of one object that share a name, and of two members of one oneof, as an AnyValue's, a converter MUST read the last [PROTOJSON]. It MUST read string_value_strindex and key_strindex, used only by Profiling, as absent, and SHOULD report their presence as a warning [OTLP].
file.4	A converter MUST write one MTSV file [MTSV-DRAFT]: every sheet of the Sheets, in their order, each with its header, and no rows where the input holds none. The Sheets run the traces tree, then the logs tree, then the eight's sheets; each sheet is followed by its lines sheets, then by its child sheets.
file.5	A line that is not a JSON value [RFC8259], a blank line being none [JSONL], or whose value is not a TracesData, MetricsData or LogsData in the OTLP JSON encoding [OTLP] [PROTOJSON], is non-conforming, as is a byte order mark at the start of the file [JSONL]. In that encoding traceId and spanId are hex strings [OTLP], other bytes are base64, a number fits its type, and a repeated field holds no null [PROTOJSON]; the keys of one list of attributes are unique [OTLP]. A line's kind of data is that of the one of resourceSpans, resourceMetrics and resourceLogs it holds, and a line that holds two, or a file whose lines hold two, is non-conforming [OTEL-FILE-EXPORTER]. A member with an unknown name does not make a line non-conforming (file.3). A converter MUST reject a file that holds such a line, and name the line, the first line being line 1 [JSONL] [RFC9413].
envelope.1	A converter MUST write the resourceSpans of a TracesData line, and the resourceLogs of a LogsData line, as the rows of a sheet of that name, their schemaUrl a column. Every row MUST carry an address: a JSON Pointer [RFC6901] to the OTLP structure the row is or belongs to, whose first reference token is the zero-based index of its line in the file and whose other tokens point within that line's JSON value, as in /0/resourceSpans/0. No two structures share an address, so the address is the key [CODD1970].
resource.1	The resource of an envelope is a member holding one object, a nonsimple domain [CODD1970], so a converter MUST write it as a child sheet of one row, keyed by its address, as in /0/resourceSpans/0/resource; an absent object, or a null one [PROTOJSON], has no row. Its attributes follow attributes.1, and its entityRefs are a child sheet, one row per element.
resource.2	A converter MUST write a list of simple values, as an EntityRef's idKeys and descriptionKeys, as a child sheet with one value per row, keyed by the address of the value.
scope.1	A converter MUST write the scopeSpans or scopeLogs of an envelope as a child sheet, one row per element, their schemaUrl a column. The scope of each is a child sheet of one row, as resource.1, with its name, version and droppedAttributesCount; its attributes follow attributes.1.
record.1	A record is a span or a log record. A converter MUST write it as a row of the spans or logRecords sheet, keyed by its address, as in /0/resourceSpans/0/scopeSpans/0/spans/0. Its simple members are columns, written as the input holds them: traceId and spanId as hex text, parentSpanId as base64 or hex text, enums as integers, 64-bit numbers and times as decimal strings [OTLP] [PROTOJSON].
span.1	A converter MUST write a span's status as a child sheet of one row, as resource.1, and its events and links as child sheets, one row per element; their attributes follow attributes.1.
attributes.1	The attributes of a resource, scope, span, span event, link or log record are a sheet of their owner. Attributes are unordered and identified by key [OTEL-COMMON], so a converter MUST key each row by the owner's address, the attribute's key, and a pointer within its value, and MUST write the value by the node rules, a row per node (node.1). A converter MUST read an attribute value as the JSON value it maps to [OTEL-COMMON]: a string a string, a boolean a boolean, an int or a double a number, except that a double of NaN, Infinity or -Infinity is the string "NaN", "Infinity" or "-Infinity", bytes a Base64 string, an empty value null, an array an array, a key-value list an object.
body.1	A log record's body is a value of any shape, read as an attribute value is (attributes.1). A converter MUST write it as one node sheet (node.1), keyed by the record's address and a pointer within the body; an absent or null body has no rows (file.3).
set.1	Each of the eight is found by its attribute key: gen_ai.system_instructions, gen_ai.tool.definitions, gen_ai.input.messages, gen_ai.output.messages, gen_ai.tool.call.arguments, gen_ai.tool.call.result, gen_ai.memory.records and gen_ai.retrieval.documents [OTEL-GENAI]. On spans and log records, and there only, a converter MUST write an attribute with one of these keys to the eight's own sheets, and never to the attributes sheet; on a resource, scope, span event or link it is an ordinary attribute. On events, which are log records with an event_name [OTLP], it is structured; on spans it is structured, or a JSON string where structure is not supported [OTEL-GENAI]. A converter MUST read both forms.
set.2	A converter MUST give each of the eight sheets of its own. Input messages and output messages are kept apart, each with its own sheets.
set.3	A value of one of the eight that does not validate against its schema [OTEL-GENAI] [JSON-SCHEMA-07], or a JSON string of one that is not a JSON value [RFC8259], is non-conforming. In validating, format is not asserted, and a keyword draft-07 does not define is ignored [JSON-SCHEMA-07]. A converter MUST reject a file that holds such a value, and name its line [RFC9413].
item.1	Every row of a relation draws on the same domains [CODD1970], so a converter MUST give a schema definition with properties of its own a sheet of its own: each part type, each tool type and each record type. A part or tool belongs to the definition its type value names when it validates against that definition [JSON-SCHEMA-07]; any other is generic, as the schemas allow [OTEL-GENAI].
item.2	A converter MUST key every row of the eight by address, the address of its span or log record (record.1), and pointer, a JSON Pointer [RFC6901] from the root of the attribute value to the row's value.
field.1	An enum (role, modality, finish_reason) holds a simple, atomic value [CODD1970], so a converter MUST write it as a column.
nested.1	A converter MUST strike a nonsimple property from its parent and write it as a child sheet that carries its parent's key [CODD1970]; normalization applies because the nesting is a tree and no key is nonsimple. A child row's pointer extends its parent's pointer, so the parent's key is carried in it.
nested.2	The rows of a relation are unordered [CODD1970], while a JSON array is ordered [RFC8259] and input messages are in the order sent [OTEL-GENAI]. A JSON Pointer names an array element by its zero-based index [RFC6901], so a converter MUST keep order in the pointer.
nested.3	Names are the sources': pointer for a JSON Pointer within a value [RFC6901], key for an attribute's key [OTLP], the OTLP JSON keys [OTLP], each schema's property names and definitions [OTEL-GENAI], type for a JSON value's type [JSON-SCHEMA-07], value for a JSON value [RFC8259], line for a line of text [MTSV-DRAFT], and address as defined in conformance.5. A converter MUST name a sheet by its parent's name, '.', and the member or definition it comes from [CODD1970]: a structure sheet's name is the full path of its OTLP JSON keys, as resourceSpans.scopeSpans.spans; a sheet of the eight starts from its attribute key alone, spans and log records both holding them; a definition is named by the type value its data carries.
node.1	A converter MUST write a value of any shape as one sheet with a row per node: its pointer, its type (null, boolean, object, array, number or string, the types of [JSON-SCHEMA-07]) and its value. The value of a string is its text, of a number its text as written, of a boolean true or false [RFC8259]; an object, an array and null have an empty value. A member whose value is null is a node of type null; an absent member has none. Rows are in the value's own order: a node before the nodes within it, members and elements as written.
node.2	Every object of the eight allows further properties [JSON-SCHEMA-07] [OTEL-GENAI]. A converter MUST write a property beyond its schema to its object's additionalProperties sheet, a node sheet named with the schemas' own keyword.
node.3	An implementation may limit the range and precision of numbers [RFC8259], so a converter MUST write a number as the input writes it.
text.1	A field is text [MTSV-DRAFT]. A property of the eight whose schema declares a default of null defaults to null [OTEL-GENAI] [JSON-SCHEMA-07]. In a column, a converter MUST write null, an absent member and the empty string as an empty field, and an empty field reads as null [CSVW].
line.1	A line break (LF, or CRLF) is the character MTSV uses for the next row [MTSV-DRAFT] [CSVW], so a field cannot hold one [MTSV-DRAFT], and a converter MUST write a text value holding one as lines: its field is left empty, and each line is a row of its lines sheet, named its sheet's name, '.', and its column (value, for a node sheet), keyed by its sheet's key with the address or pointer extended to the text value, and line, the line's zero-based position. A line is the text between line breaks, so the lines joined with LF give the text back, a CRLF reading back as LF. Every column the source types as string, and every node sheet's value, has a lines sheet, which follows its sheet in the order of its columns; ids, times, counts, flags and enums have none.
character.1	A field that holds HT, LF, FF or CR cannot be represented, a generator MUST NOT write it, and its handling is left to the application [MTSV-DRAFT]. A converter MUST leave such a field empty. A line break is carried as lines (line.1); HT, FF and a CR alone are not carried.
